Here's the CartComponent implementation leveraging the Redux slice:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from 'react-redux';
import { addItem, removeItem } from './cartSlice';

const CartComponent = () => {
  const { items } = useSelector(state => state.cart);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  const handleAddToCart = (product) => {
    dispatch(addItem({ ...product, quantity: 1 }));
  };

  const handleRemoveFromCart = (id) => {
    dispatch(removeItem(id));
  };

  return (
    <div className="cart">
      <h2>Shopping Cart</h2>
      {items.length === 0 ? (
        <p>Your cart is empty</p>
      ) : (
        <ul className="cart-items">
          {items.map(item => (
            <li key={item.id} className="cart-item">
              <img src={item.image} alt={item.name} />
              <div className="details">
                <h3>{item.name}</h3>
                <p>${item.price}</p>
                <p>Qty: {item.quantity}</p>
              </div>
              <button onClick={() => handleRemoveFromCart(item.id)}>
                Remove
              </button>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
      <button 
        className="checkout-btn" 
        disabled={items.length === 0}
        onClick={() => alert('Checkout process would start here')}
      >
        Checkout ({items.length} items)
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default CartComponent;
```

This component uses `useSelector` to read the cart state and `useDispatch` to trigger actions. The `handleAddToCart` uses the `addItem` action creator you already defined in the slice. You'd hook this component into your application's main view through the Redux Provider setup.

